        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Karnak</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1971 BCE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Made by</span><span class="info-stat-val">Built over many centuries by about thirty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-i">Senusret I</a> of the Middle Kingdom into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Kingdom</a>, though most surviving buildings date from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>; later cultures adapted some of its buildings for their own worship</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Form</span><span class="info-stat-val">A mix of temples, pylons, chapels and other buildings in four main parts: the Precincts of Amun-Re, Mut and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montu">Montu</a> and the dismantled Temple of Amenhotep IV; the Great Hypostyle Hall has 134 columns on an area of 5,000 square metres</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where it is now</span><span class="info-stat-val">Near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxor">Luxor</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, beside the modern village of el-Karnak, 2.5 kilometres north of Luxor; part of the monumental city of Thebes, listed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> in 1979</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the Great Hypostyle Hall with its 134 columns; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisks</a>; the Red Chapel; the Avenue of Sphinxes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxor">Luxor Temple</a>; a building history of some thirty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a>; Coptic churches founded among the ruins; the sacred precincts of Amun-Re, Mut and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montu">Montu</a></span></div>
