    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Karnak was the ancient Egyptian Ipet-isut, 'The Most Selected of Places', and the main place of worship of the Theban Triad, with the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> as its head.</li><li>Construction began under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-i">Senusret I</a> in the Middle Kingdom and continued into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Kingdom</a>; about thirty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> contributed, which gave the complex a size, complexity and diversity not seen elsewhere.</li><li>After the closing of pagan temples in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, Karnak was mostly abandoned and Christian churches were founded among the ruins; Europeans described it from 1589, and it was listed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> with the rest of Thebes in 1979.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut</a> had twin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisks</a> erected at the entrance to the temple; one still stands, and the other has toppled and is broken.</p></aside>
    </section>

