    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">20th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Construction at the complex began during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-i">Senusret I</a> (1971–1926 BCE) in the Middle Kingdom; Thebes later became the capital of the unified country, and almost every <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty</a> added something to the site</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">356</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-ii">Constantius II</a> ordered the closing of pagan temples throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, and Karnak, by then mostly abandoned, received Christian churches among its ruins</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1589</span><span class="rf-text">An unknown Venetian wrote the first European description of the temple complex, without giving it a name</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1668</span><span class="rf-text">Two Capuchin missionary brothers travelled through the area, and the name Karnak is first attested as a village name and the name of the complex</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1704</span><span class="rf-text">Paul Lucas published the first drawing of Karnak, which is rather inaccurate</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1979</span><span class="rf-text">Karnak was added to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage List along with the rest of the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/senusret-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/senusret-i.jpg" alt="Senusret I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/senusret-i">Senusret I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_Senusret_I_Petrie_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">W. M. Flinders Petrie (1853-1942), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The history of the Karnak complex is largely the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> and its changing role in the culture. Religious centres varied by region, and when a new capital of the unified culture was established, the religious centres in that area gained prominence. The city of Thebes does not appear to have been of great significance before the Eleventh Dynasty, and earlier temple building there would have been relatively small, with shrines dedicated to the early deities of Thebes, the earth goddess Mut and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montu">Montu</a>. Early building was destroyed by invaders.</p><p>The earliest known artefact found in the area of the temple is a small, eight-sided column from the Eleventh Dynasty, which mentions Amun-Re. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>, sometimes called Amen, was long the local tutelary deity of Thebes. He was identified with the ram and the goose, and his name means 'hidden' or 'the hidden god'. Construction at the complex began during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-i">Senusret I</a>, who reigned 1971–1926 BCE, in the Middle Kingdom. Major construction in the Precinct of Amun-Re took place in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty</a>, when Thebes became the capital of the unified country.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pinedjem-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pinedjem-i.jpg" alt="Pinedjem I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pinedjem-i">Pinedjem I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pinedjem_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Juan R. Lazaro, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Karnak comprises a vast mix of temples, pylons, chapels and other buildings. It has four main parts: the Precinct of Amun-Re, the Precinct of Mut, the Precinct of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montu">Montu</a> and the dismantled Temple of Amenhotep IV. The term Karnak is often understood as the Precinct of Amun-Re only. That is the largest precinct and is dedicated to Amun-Re, the chief deity of the Theban Triad. It holds several colossal statues, including a figure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pinedjem-i">Pinedjem I</a> that is 10.5 metres tall, and one of the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisks</a>, weighing 328 tons and standing 29 metres tall.</p><p>The Great Hypostyle Hall in this precinct has an area of 5,000 square metres with 134 massive columns arranged in 16 rows. One hundred and twenty-two of them are 10 metres tall, and the other 12 are 21 metres tall with a diameter of over 3 metres. The sun god's shrine was built so that light is focused upon it during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-solstice">winter solstice</a>. The Precinct of Mut, to the south, was dedicated to the mother goddess Mut, identified in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty</a> as the wife of Amun-Re, and has its own crescent-shaped sacred lake. The Precinct of Montu, to the north, honours the war god Montu, the son of Mut and Amun-Re, and is much smaller.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thutmose-i.jpg" alt="Thutmose I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-i">Thutmose I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Egyptian_Sandstone_Head_of_Thutmose_I,_Karnak_(Thebes),_18th_Dynasty_(35727582483).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gary Todd from Xinzheng, China, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-i">Thutmose I</a> erected an enclosure wall connecting the Fourth and Fifth pylons, which make up the earliest part of the temple still standing in situ. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut</a> had monuments constructed and restored the original Precinct of Mut, which had been ravaged by the foreign rulers during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> occupation. She also had the twin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisks</a> erected, and her Red Chapel, intended as a barque shrine, may originally have stood between them. She later ordered two more obelisks to mark her sixteenth year as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>; one broke during construction, so a third was made to replace it. The broken one remains at its quarry in Aswan as the unfinished obelisk, which shows how obelisks were quarried.</p><p>Construction of the Great Hypostyle Hall may have begun in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty</a>, although most new building was undertaken under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seti-i">Seti I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> in the Nineteenth. The sandstone for the Precinct of Amun-Re, including all the columns, was brought from Gebel Silsila, 100 miles south on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>. The architraves on the columns are estimated to weigh 70 tons. They may have been lifted with levers, or towed up ramps of sand, mud, brick or stone; a common alternative theory favours the ramps. An unfinished pillar shows that final carving was done after the drums were in place.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/merneptah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/merneptah.jpg" alt="Merneptah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/merneptah">Merneptah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ramses_and_the_Pharaohs%27_Gold,_London_2026-03-06_-_149_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hyspaosines, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Karnak is distinguished from most other Egyptian temples by the length of time over which it was developed and used. Approximately thirty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> contributed to the buildings. Few of its individual features are unique, but their size and number are vast, and the deities represented range from some of the earliest worshipped to some of those worshipped much later in Egyptian history. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merneptah">Merneptah</a> of the Nineteenth Dynasty commemorated his victories over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-peoples">Sea Peoples</a> on the walls of the Cachette Court, the start of the processional route, also called the Avenue of Sphinxes, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxor">Luxor Temple</a>.</p><p>Not every ruler built in the same spirit. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a>, or Amenhotep IV, built a temple east of the main complex, outside the walls of the Amun-Re precinct. He had attempted to overcome the powerful priesthood that had gained control over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> before his reign. The temple was destroyed immediately after his death and so thoroughly demolished that its full extent and layout are unknown. The priesthood regained its position as soon as he died and was instrumental in destroying many records of his existence. Ancient Greek and Roman writers wrote about monuments in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, Karnak among them.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nectanebo-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nectanebo-i.jpg" alt="Nectanebo I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nectanebo-i">Nectanebo I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nectanebo_I_with_khepresh_crown.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Marco Prins, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The last major change to the layout of the Precinct of Amun-Re was the addition of the First Pylon and the massive enclosure walls around the precinct, both built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nectanebo-i">Nectanebo I</a> of the Thirtieth Dynasty. In 323 CE the Roman emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a> recognised the Christian religion, and in 356 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-ii">Constantius II</a> ordered the closing of pagan temples throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, into which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> had been annexed in 30 BCE. Karnak was by then mostly abandoned. Christian churches were founded among the ruins; the most famous example is the reuse of the central hall of the Festival Hall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii">Thutmose III</a>, where painted decorations of saints and Coptic inscriptions can still be seen.</p><p>The exact position of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> was unknown in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> both give it. Several European authors of the 15th and 16th centuries who visited only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a> put Thebes in or near Memphis. The first European description of the complex was by an unknown Venetian in 1589. The name Karnak is first attested in 1668, in the travels of the Capuchin brothers Protais and Charles François d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>. Paul Lucas's drawing of 1704 mixed the Precincts of Amun-Re and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montu">Montu</a>. Later visitors included Richard Pococke, James Bruce and scientists of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> expedition, among them Vivant Denon, in 1798–1799.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1979 Karnak was added to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage List along with the rest of the monumental city of Thebes. It takes its modern name from the nearby village of el-Karnak, which means 'fortified village', in place of the ancient name Ipet-isut. Its long history has made it a record of adaptation, since later cultures used the buildings of the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> for their own religious purposes, as in the Coptic churches.</p><p>Research there has continued. Betsy Bryan led a Johns Hopkins University team that excavated and restored the Precinct of Mut, where six hundred black granite statues were found in the courtyard of the temple. In 2006 she presented findings about a festival that included apparent intentional overindulgence in alcohol, in which the priestesses and the population took part, and historical records speak of tens of thousands attending. The festival is linked to the myth of Sekhmet, whom Ra tricked into drinking beer mixed with pomegranate juice that looked like blood. In 2009 UCLA launched a website dedicated to virtual reality digital reconstructions of the complex and other resources.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Karnak, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxor">Luxor</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, is a vast complex of temples, pylons and chapels begun under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-i">Senusret I</a> and added to by about thirty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> into Ptolemaic times. It was the main place of worship of the Theban Triad headed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>, and later served Christian communities.</em></p>

